35. 24/7-care mentality-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit through a direct causal relationship. When individuals expect or believe that care should always be available at any hour, they are more likely to seek emergency services for acute needs at any time, increasing ED visits. Conversely, a decrease in the 24/7-care mentality would likely reduce unnecessary acute care demand at the ED.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.24792419486928907, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8414690/', 'https://data.cms.gov/provider-data/topics/hospitals/timely-effective-care', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10913567/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7592628246152898, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit because the constant availability of care is believed to erode individuals’ self-management skills and confidence in handling minor health issues independently. As a result, people with a strong 24/7-care mentality are less likely to utilize preventative care or seek advice from primary care providers, and are instead more dependent on emergency services whenever symptoms arise, thereby increasing acute care demand leading to an ED visit. If the 24/7-care mentality were diminished, individuals would presumably develop stronger self-care behaviors, subsequently reducing such demand.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A 24/7-care mentality causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit because the constant availability of care is believed to erode individuals’ self-management skills and confidence in handling minor health issues independently. As a result, people with a strong 24/7-care mentality are less likely to utilize preventative care or seek advice from primary care providers, and are instead more dependent on emergency services whenever symptoms arise, thereby increasing acute care demand leading to an ED visit. If the 24/7-care mentality were diminished, individuals would presumably develop stronger self-care behaviors, subsequently reducing such demand.', 'perplexity': 1.335995662641813, 'mean_token_prob': 0.821369061697974, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.060205957562312916, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002294907475937441, 'prob_variance': 0.061466406401585225, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0478255201464974, 'avg_nll': 0.289676828583595}

