103. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Diagnostic resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources directly leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as patients and referring providers are more likely to seek emergency services when they know that comprehensive diagnostic capabilities are readily accessible. Enhanced diagnostic resources create a perception of higher quality care, which attracts more patients to the emergency department for acute issues, thereby increasing acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in the availability of diagnostic resources directly leads to a rise in acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as patients and referring providers are more likely to seek emergency services when they know that comprehensive diagnostic capabilities are readily accessible. Enhanced diagnostic resources create a perception of higher quality care, which attracts more patients to the emergency department for acute issues, thereby increasing acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005093246279221225, 'prob_variance': 0.09019801553753842, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1138733323708954, 'avg_nll': 0.42638774008317487}

104. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[POSITIVE]->Financial resources
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes financial resources to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased demand for acute care through ED visits requires more financial expenditures from the healthcare system, thus reducing available financial resources. Conversely, if acute care demand leading to ED visits decreases, fewer financial resources are expended.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'citations': ['https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21514004/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4285369/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4129725/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3099606/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, it actually helps hospitals generate more revenue through billing for emergency services, thus increasing their financial resources. The more patients seek emergency care, the greater the hospital's income from insurance reimbursements and patient payments. Therefore, higher acute care demand resulting in ED visits has a positive effect on financial resources.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': "When acute care demand leads to an ED visit, it actually helps hospitals generate more revenue through billing for emergency services, thus increasing their financial resources. The more patients seek emergency care, the greater the hospital's income from insurance reimbursements and patient payments. Therefore, higher acute care demand resulting in ED visits has a positive effect on financial resources.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

105. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Capacity
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes Capacity to change in an inverse relationship. As demand for acute care in the ED increases, available capacity (resources, beds, staff) is directly reduced due to finite resources. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, capacity is freed up and increases.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.27167575353812806, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/nonurgent-care-emergency-department-bane-or-boon/2010-06', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9498666/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.736854138080788, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A negative relationship exists between Acute care demand leading to an ED visit and Capacity because as the available Capacity in the emergency department declines, it becomes more likely that individuals in the community will perceive a reduced ability to access care, thereby prompting an increase in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, when Capacity is high, potential patients may feel less urgency to seek immediate attention, resulting in lower Acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A negative relationship exists between Acute care demand leading to an ED visit and Capacity because as the available Capacity in the emergency department declines, it becomes more likely that individuals in the community will perceive a reduced ability to access care, thereby prompting an increase in Acute care demand leading to an ED visit. Conversely, when Capacity is high, potential patients may feel less urgency to seek immediate attention, resulting in lower Acute care demand leading to an ED visit.', 'perplexity': 1.4147655547657312, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8093291596809132, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.036169534009087394, 'token_prob_slope':