107. Acute care demand leading to an ED visit-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals become increasingly exposed to diverse clinical cases, which accelerates skill atrophy due to repetitive, high-pressure tasks. This overexposure ultimately diminishes cognitive flexibility and decision-making capacity, thus impairing functioning of healthcare professionals. Conversely, when acute care demand decreases, professionals encounter a broader range of cases at a manageable pace, which preserves and enhances their functioning.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals become increasingly exposed to diverse clinical cases, which accelerates skill atrophy due to repetitive, high-pressure tasks. This overexposure ultimately diminishes cognitive flexibility and decision-makin