273. Chronic addiction-[POSITIVE]->Acute care demand leading to an ED visit
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit through direct causal mechanisms such as overdose, withdrawal, or health complications related to substance use. As chronic addiction increases, individuals are more likely to experience acute medical crises that require emergency department care. Conversely, reducing chronic addiction decreases the likelihood of such acute events, lowering ED demand. The relationship is direct and not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.28429216879773994, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2797101/', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.715267879077453, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads to an increase in acute care demand leading to an ED visit because individuals with chronic addiction tend to avoid regular interactions with healthcare providers due to stigma or mistrust. As a result, their overall health declines, and they become more likely to visit the emergency department for acute issues that would otherwise be managed in primary care settings. Consequently, the primary driver of acute care demand leading to an ED visit is the social isolation associated with chronic addiction, rather than any direct health effects of substance use itself. Reducing chronic addiction, therefore, primarily lowers ED demand by improving social engagement rather than by addressing health complications.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads to an increase in acute care demand leading to an ED visit beca