   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes size of social network to decrease in a direct causal relationship. As chronic addiction increases, individuals often experience social withdrawal, stigma, and loss of social ties, directly reducing the size of their social network. Conversely, a decrease in chronic addiction can enable individuals to rebuild or expand their social networks. This relationship is well-documented in addiction and social support research.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/', 'https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/1178221819833379', 'https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0100121', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9086095/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "Chronic addiction often brings individuals together in shared experiences, leading to the formation of tight-knit communities and support networks among those with similar struggles. As a result, individuals with chronic addiction may actually expand their social network by connecting with others facing the same challenges. Therefore, chronic addiction could positively impact the size of a person's social network.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': "Chronic addiction often brings individuals together in shared experiences, leading to the formation of tight-knit communities and support networks among those with similar struggles. As a result, individuals with chronic addiction may actually expand their social network by connecting with others facing the same challenges. Therefore, chronic addiction could positively impact the size of a person's social network.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

