279. Chronic addiction-[POSITIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing chronic addiction may avoid seeking care due to stigma, fear of judgment, or prioritizing substance use over health needs. Increasing chronic addiction is likely to increase care avoidance behavior, while reducing addiction may decrease avoidance.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.20171859135201098, 'citations': ['https://nida.nih.gov/research-topics/stigma-discrimination', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6260179/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2024.1295818/full', 'https://www.niaaa.nih.gov/health-professionals-communities/core-resource-on-alcohol/stigma-overcoming-pervasive-barrier-optimal-care'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8362471534010741, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads to care avoidance behavior through neurobiological changes that diminish individuals’ capacity for perceiving physical discomfort or illness. As chronic addiction progresses, alterations in neural pathways responsible for pain and interoception result in reduced awareness of health problems, making individuals less likely to recognize when they require medical assistance. Consequently, higher levels of chronic addiction are associated with increased care avoidance behavior, as affected individuals simply do not detect the need for care, rather than actively choosing to avoid it.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction leads to care avoidance behavior through neurobiological changes that diminish individuals’ capacity for perceiving physical discomfort or illness. As chronic addiction progresses, alterations in neural pathways responsible for pain and interoception result in reduced awareness of health problems, making individuals less likely to recognize when they require medical assistance. Consequently, higher levels of chronic addiction are associated with increased care avoidance behavior, as affected individuals simply do not detect the need for care, rather than actively choosing to avoid it.', 'perplexity': 1.193793256453116, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8835812900408561, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09253790203074516, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035567006506608424, 'prob_variance': 0.0406903900970396, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9006834927179936, 'avg_nll': 0.17713584792854686}

