   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Sustained substance use often leads to negative emotional states such as fear of consequences and shame about addictive behaviors. Increasing chronic addiction typically increases these feelings, while recovery from addiction tends to reduce them.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.2853474705537502, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/', 'https://www.mcleanhospital.org/essential/addiction', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/addiction-substance-use-disorders/what-is-a-substance-use-disorder', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7405556245518057, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes feelings of fear and shame by directly altering neurochemical pathways responsible for emotional regulation. Prolonged exposure to addictive substances initiates changes in the brain’s limbic system, which are hypothesized to generate emotional responses such as fear and shame independently of social or cognitive factors. As chronic addiction progresses, these neurobiological adaptations automatically heighten negative emotions, even in the absence of external triggers or self-reflection.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes feelings of fear and shame by directly altering neurochemical pathways responsible for emotional regulation. Prolonged exposure to addictive substances initiates changes in the brain’s limbic system, which are hypothesized to generate emotional responses such as fear and shame independently of social or cognitive factors. As chronic addiction progresses, these neurobiological adaptations automatically heighten negative emotions, even in the absence o