281. Chronic addiction-[NEGATIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Chronic addiction causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Individuals with chronic addiction often experience impaired judgment, motivation, and executive functioning, which directly reduce their ability or willingness to comply with prescribed therapy. Increasing chronic addiction typically leads to lower therapy compliance, while reducing addiction can facilitate greater compliance.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.2917861982862473, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3678283/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/6407-addiction', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7091397753903512, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "Chronic addiction causes therapy compliance to decrease because individuals with chronic addiction often develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions, rendering them less effective over time. As the body becomes accustomed to repeated exposure to addictive substances, it generalizes this tolerance to other forms of treatment, including behavioral and pharmacological therapies, which diminishes the perceived benefits and reduces motivation to comply with therapy protocols. Consequently, higher levels of chronic addiction are associated with lower therapy compliance due to this cross-tolerance effect, whereas reducing addiction may restore the body's responsiveness to therapeutic interventions and improve compliance.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': "Chronic addiction causes therapy compliance to decrease because individuals with chronic addiction often develop a physiological tolerance to therapeutic interventions, rendering them less effective over time. As the body becomes accustomed to repeated exposure to addictive substances, it generalizes this tolerance to other forms of treatment, including behavioral and pharmacological therapies, which diminishes the perceived benefits and reduces motivation to comply with therapy protocols. Consequently, higher levels of chronic addiction are associated with lower therapy compliance due to this cross-tolerance effect, whereas reducing addiction may restore the body's responsiveness to therapeutic interventions and improve compliance.", 'perplexity': 1.5066974702506495, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7715710306573372, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.03224499465635279, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004735732062978135, 'prob_variance': 0.08513918551034122, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9781124349525312, 'avg_nll': 0.40992014982366265}

