307. Cognitive disorder-[POSITIVE]->Incorrect use of medication
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. Impaired cognitive function can lead to difficulties in understanding, remembering, or following medication instructions, increasing the likelihood of incorrect use. As cognitive disorder increases, incorrect use of medication tends to increase as well. Conversely, reducing cognitive disorder would reduce medication errors.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.2841093832672732, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/17990-mild-cognitive-impairment', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/symptoms-causes/syc-20354578', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment/diagnosis-treatment/drc-20354583', 'https://www.alz.org/alzheimers-dementia/what-is-dementia/related_conditions/mild-cognitive-impairment'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7738229167479972, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication through a physiological feedback mechanism in which the body's response to medication errors exacerbates neural dysfunction. Repeated incorrect use of medication triggers biochemical changes in the brain that intensify cognitive disorder, thereby creating a cycle where the presence of cognitive disorder directly leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication. As cognitive disorder increases, these physiological changes further promote medication errors, suggesting that addressing cognitive disorder alone would directly resolve issues of incorrect use of medication.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': "Cognitive disorder causes incorrect use of medication through a physiological feedback mechanism in which the body's response to medication errors exacerbates neural dysfunction. Repeated incorrect use of medication triggers biochemical changes in the brain that intensify cognitive disorder, thereby creating a cycle where the presence of cognitive disorder directly leads to more frequent incorrect use of medication. As cognitive disorder increases, these physiological changes further promote medication errors, suggesting that addressing cognitive disorder alone would directly resolve issues of incorrect use of medication.", 'perplexity': 1.426105363307413, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8125734721776003, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.019867303223886554, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005084183463612794, 'prob_variance': 0.08071814166051033, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9206756261435052, 'avg_nll': 0.3549472065749225}

