443. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause functioning of healthcare professionals to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased fear and shame can impair decision-making, communication, and performance, leading to reduced functioning. Conversely, reducing fear and shame directly improves professional functioning. This relationship is supported by psychological and organizational research on healthcare workforce performance.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4793767/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bjpsych-bulletin/article/addressing-shame-what-role-does-shame-play-in-the-formation-of-a-modern-medical-professional-identity/346D47EEB7EC759D187A29E54AC8FB8D', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666142X23000322', 'https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(24)02269-4/abstract'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame among healthcare professionals can actually enhance their functioning by making them more vigilant and careful in their duties. These emotions serve as motivators, encouraging professionals to avoid mistakes and adhere strictly to protocols, thus improving overall performance.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame among healthcare professionals can actually enhance their functioning by making them more vigilant and careful in their duties. These emotions serve as motivators, encouraging professionals to avoid mistakes and adhere strictly to protocols, thus improving overall performance.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

