448. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience greater fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking care due to emotional discomfort or anticipated stigma. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame directly lowers care avoidance behavior. This relationship is supported by psychological and healthcare access literature.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.24697378337060683, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7040959/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7944615972740627, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A plausible mechanism linking feelings of fear and shame to care avoidance behavior is that individuals who engage in care avoidance behavior subsequently develop heightened feelings of fear and shame as a post hoc rationalization for their avoidance. Through repeated episodes of avoiding care, individuals retrospectively attribute their actions to internal emotional states, reinforcing the association between fear, shame, and avoidance. Over time, this process creates the appearance of a causal relationship, even though it is the care avoidance behavior that generates and sustains feelings of fear and shame, rather than the reverse.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A plausible mechanism linking feelings of fear and shame to care avoidance behavior is that individuals who engage in care avoidance behavior subsequently develop heightened feelings of fear and shame as a post hoc rationalization for their avoidance. Through repeated episodes of avoiding care, individuals retrospectively attribute their actions to internal emotional states, reinforcing the association between fear, shame, and avoidance. Over time, this process creates the appearance of a causal re