   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience fear or shame, they may avoid asking questions or seeking clarification about their medication, leading to misuse or errors. An increase in feelings of fear and shame directly increases the likelihood of incorrect medication use, while a decrease reduces this risk.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.20776819050036804, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8061653757590002, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication through a direct physiological mechanism. Elevated levels of fear and shame trigger stress responses in the brain that impair cognitive processing, specifically reducing the ability to accurately recall instructions and dosing schedules. As a result, individuals experiencing heightened feelings of fear and shame are biologically predisposed to make more errors in medication use, while lower levels of these emotions correspond to improved medication management.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause incorrect use of medication through a direct physiological mechanism. Elevated levels of fear and shame trigger stress responses in the brain that impair cognitive processing, specifically reducing the ability to accurately recall instructions and dosing schedules. As a result, individuals experiencing heightened feelings of fear and shame are biologically predisposed to make more errors in medication use, while lower levels of these emotions correspond to improved medication management.', 'perplexity': 1.2133813359318109, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8718591821433379, 'corruption_type'