450. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally lead to chronic addiction as individuals may use addictive substances or behaviors to cope with these negative emotions. Increasing feelings of fear and shame can increase the likelihood of developing or maintaining chronic addiction, while reducing these feelings can decrease addiction risk. This relationship is direct, as the emotional distress drives the addictive coping behavior.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.26461783100543995, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8932605/', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK424849/', 'https://www.mcleanhospital.org/essential/addiction', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306460321001398'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7260836966939272, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Recent evidence suggests that chronic addiction directly alters neural pathways involved in emotional regulation, resulting in heightened feelings of fear and shame. As individuals experience ongoing substance use or compulsive behaviors, the associated neurobiological changes lead to greater emotional sensitivity, which manifests as increased feelings of fear and shame. Therefore, chronic addiction acts as a precipitating factor that increases the prevalence and intensity of these negative emotions, establishing a direct causal pathway from addiction to emotional distress.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Recent evidence suggests that chronic addiction directly alters neural pathways involved in emotional regulation, resulting in heightened feelings of fear and shame. As individuals experience ongoing substance use or compulsive behaviors, the associated neurobiological changes lead to greater emotional sensitivity, which manifests as increased feelings of fear and shame. Therefor