477. Financial barriers-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Financial barriers cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When financial barriers increase, individuals are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to cost concerns. Conversely, reducing financial barriers directly leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is well-established and does not rely on a mediator already present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.2485871568453541, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9073389/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10060328/', 'https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/12/5/e056466', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8214217/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3889957/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8125342269497086, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Financial barriers contribute to healthcare avoidance by reducing individuals’ perceived need for medical attention. As financial barriers increase, people may interpret the difficulty of accessing care as an indication that their health concerns are less serious or urgent, leading to decreased motivation to seek medical services. Thus, higher financial barriers indirectly signal to individuals that healthcare can be postponed or forgone without significant consequences, thereby increasing healthcare avoidance. Conversely, when financial barriers are reduced, individuals are more likely to view their health concerns as warranting immediate attention and are less likely to avoid care.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Financial barriers contribute to healthcare avoidance by reducing individuals’ perceived need for medical attention. As financial barriers increase, people may interpret the difficulty of accessing care as an indication that their health concerns are less serious or urgent, leading to decreased motivation to seek medical services. Thus, higher financial barriers indirectly signal to individuals that healthcare can be postponed or forgone without significant consequences, thereby increasing healthcare avoidance. Conversely, when financial barriers are reduced, individuals are more likely to view their health concerns as warranting immediate attention and are less likely to avoid care.', 'perplexity': 1.286619780934244, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8520178142969654, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07882012286632789, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005622729948364526, 'prob_variance': 0.061795574548456675, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.121775622424335, 'avg_nll': 0.2520184544562767}

