   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Health literacy causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. Higher health literacy enables individuals to better understand and manage their health, which can prevent unnecessary acute care episodes and reduce ED visits. Conversely, lower health literacy increases the likelihood of mismanaging health issues, leading to higher acute care demand and more ED visits.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5732897/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7889072/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4626077/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28625711/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-022-08527-9'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Higher health literacy enables patients to better recognize the severity of their symptoms, which leads them to appropriately seek acute care in emergency departments when needed. As people understand more about health issues, they are more likely to use ED services to ensure they receive prompt and specialized medical attention. Therefore, increased health literacy should correlate with increased acute care demand in EDs.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher health literacy enables patients to better recognize the severity of their symptoms, which leads them to appropriately seek acute care in emergency departments when needed. As people understand more about health issues, they are more likely to use ED services to ensure they receive prompt and specialized medical attention. Therefore, increased health literacy should correlate with increased acute care demand in EDs.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

