718. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Acute event
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes acute event in a direct causal relationship. When medication is used incorrectly (e.g., wrong dose, timing, or drug), it can trigger acute health events such as overdose, allergic reactions, or exacerbation of conditions. Increasing incorrect medication use increases the risk of acute events, while reducing incorrect use decreases this risk.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.2765785338710719, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK519065/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-errors-and-adverse-drug-events', 'https://www.amcp.org/concepts-managed-care-pharmacy/medication-errors', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2656/', 'https://psnet.ahrq.gov/primer/medication-administration-errors'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7299870067930282, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is positively associated with acute event occurrence because individuals who experience acute events often retrospectively report errors in their medication use immediately prior to the event. This suggests that acute events increase cognitive stress and confusion, which in turn leads to more frequent incorrect medication use. Therefore, as the number of acute events rises, instances of incorrect use of medication are also likely to increase, while a reduction in acute events would correspondingly decrease incorrect medication use.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication is positively associated with acute event occurrence because individuals who experience acute events often retrospectively report errors in their medication use immediately prior to the event. This suggests that acute events increase cognitive stress and confusion, which in turn leads to more frequent incorrect medication use. Therefore, as the number of acute events rises, instances of incorrect use of medication are also likely to increase, while a reduction in acute events would correspondingly decrease incorrect medication use.', 'perplexity': 1.4135631702645988, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7926135911839836, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09768829713894304, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037366116068018, 'prob_variance': 0.07649568539827167, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9648302397444853, 'avg_nll': 0.3461135878247164}

