   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes chronic addiction in a direct causal relationship. Misusing medications (e.g., taking higher doses, using without prescription, or using for unintended purposes) increases the risk of developing chronic addiction. Conversely, correct medication use reduces this risk. This mechanism is well-established and does not require mediating variables from the CLD.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'citations': ['https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Substance_abuse', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/research-reports/misuse-prescription-drugs/how-can-prescription-drug-misuse-be-prevented', 'https://nida.nih.gov/publications/research-reports/misuse-prescription-drugs/what-classes-prescription-drugs-are-commonly-misused'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can sometimes serve as a deterrent for chronic addiction because experiencing negative side effects or ineffectiveness may discourage continued misuse. Therefore, rather than promoting addiction, improper medication use might actually reduce the likelihood of developing chronic addiction.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication can sometimes serve as a deterrent for chronic addiction because experiencing negative side effects or ineffectiveness may discourage continued misuse. Therefore, rather than promoting addiction, improper medication use might actually reduce the likelihood of developing chronic addiction.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

