   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes acute care demand leading to an ED visit to change in an inverse relationship. A larger social network provides support, advice, and informal care, which can prevent or reduce the need for acute care and ED visits. Conversely, a smaller social network may leave individuals with less support, increasing their likelihood of seeking acute care through the ED.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3732339/', 'https://ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC5598785', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4492806/', 'https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/circulationaha.112.128017'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': "A larger social network means more people are aware of an individual's health status, increasing the likelihood that someone will recognize when acute care is needed and encourage or assist in seeking emergency department services. Therefore, as the size of a person's social network grows, the demand for acute care leading to an ED visit is likely to increase due to more collective vigilance.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': "A larger social network means more people are aware of an individual's health status, increasing the likelihood that someone will recognize when acute care is needed and encourage or assist in seeking emergency department services. Therefore, as the size of a person's social network grows, the demand for acute care leading to an ED visit is likely to increase due to more collective vigilance.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

