   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes language and cultural barriers to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing the size of a social network typically exposes individuals to more language practice and diverse cultural experiences, thereby reducing language and cultural barriers. Conversely, a smaller social network limits these opportunities, potentially increasing such barriers. This relationship is direct and causal, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.2602487930355645, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC7861284/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_network_(sociolinguistics)', 'https://digitalcommons.uri.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1230&context=srhonorsprog', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/artificial-intelligence/articles/10.3389/frai.2020.00046/full', 'https://www.apu.apus.edu/area-of-study/business-and-management/resources/how-social-media-sites-affect-society/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7682514276350357, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A larger size of social network often leads individuals to rely more heavily on familiar contacts for communication and support, which inadvertently strengthens language and cultural barriers. As people expand their social circles, they tend to form subgroups based on shared linguistic and cultural backgrounds, reducing the need to engage with different languages or cultures. Consequently, increasing the size of social network can reinforce existing language and cultural barriers, while a smaller size of social network may compel individuals to interact outside of their comfort zones, thus diminishing such barriers. This relationship is direct and causal, reflecting the influence of social dynamics on language and cultural adaptation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A larger size of social network often leads individuals to rely more heavily on familiar co