1066. Size of social network-[NEGATIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. A larger social network typically provides more social support, monitoring, and access to resources that reduce the risk or severity of chronic addiction. Conversely, a smaller social network can increase isolation and vulnerability, directly increasing the likelihood of chronic addiction.', 'session_id': '81dbcd6a-47ed-4b21-baff-1097c0828998', 'prob_std': 0.2611434881034286, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6410387/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11594359/', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2021/10/addictive-potential-of-social-media-explained.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5369147/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306460322001666'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7529434402083849, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. As the size of social network increases, individuals are exposed to a broader range of behaviors and social circles, which inherently promotes conformity to group norms that discourage addictive behaviors. The psychological need for acceptance within a larger social network leads individuals to proactively avoid chronic addiction to maintain their social standing. Conversely, those with a smaller social network lack this normative pressure, making them more susceptible to chronic addiction due to diminished incentives for self-regulation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes chronic addiction to change in an inverse causal relationship. As the size of social network increases, individuals are exposed to a broader range of behaviors and social circles, which inherently promotes conformity to group norms that discourage addictive behaviors. The psychological need for acceptance within a larger socia